    "reconciliation_findings": {
      "mathematical_impossibility_explained": "5,622 flows each having >= 2 physical packets would require at least 11,244 packets, which is impossible in a 6,554-packet pcap. In reality, 5,346 of the 5,622 attack flows contain exactly 1 physical packet in the pcap (single SYN without retransmission).",
      "python_port_bug": "In cicflowmeter/flow_session.py, a new flow is instantiated via Flow(pkt), which adds pkt to self.packets in Flow.__init__. Immediately following this block, execution falls through to flow.add_packet(pkt), appending the very same packet a second time. Consequently, every single-packet flow is assigned tot_fwd_pkts = 2, and every 2-packet retransmission is assigned tot_fwd_pkts = 3. Furthermore, the Python port extracts packet length as wire/IP packet length (54 bytes) rather than TCP payload length, giving 54 * 2 = 108 bytes.",
      "java_cicflowmeter_behavior": "Java CICFlowMeter requires bidirectional activity or >= 2 packets to output a flow, completely discarding all 5,346 single-packet flows. It outputs exactly 277 flows (276 flows with >= 2 physical SYN packets due to retransmissions, plus 1 single-packet flow that received a SYN-ACK reply). Java measures Total Length of Fwd Packets strictly as TCP payload length, which is 0 for pure SYNs."
